WEBVTT

00:00:00.015 --> 00:00:02.729
The Gift of the Magi, by O. Henry.

00:00:02.729 --> 00:00:05.210
One dollar and eighty-seven cents.

00:00:05.210 --> 00:00:06.263
That was all.

00:00:06.263 --> 00:00:08.873
And sixty cents of it was in pennies.

00:00:08.873 --> 00:00:20.923
Pennies saved one and two at a time by bulldozing the grocer and the vegetable man and the butcher until one’s cheeks burned with the silent imputation of parsimony that such close dealing implied.

00:00:20.923 --> 00:00:23.032
Three times Della counted it.

00:00:23.032 --> 00:00:25.513
One dollar and eighty-seven cents.

00:00:25.513 --> 00:00:27.631
And the next day would be Christmas.

00:00:27.631 --> 00:00:32.951
There was clearly nothing to do but flop down on the shabby little couch and howl.

00:00:32.951 --> 00:00:34.266
So Della did it.

00:00:34.266 --> 00:00:42.374
Which instigates the moral reflection that life is made up of sobs, sniffles, and smiles, with sniffles predominating.

00:00:42.374 --> 00:00:49.108
While the mistress of the home is gradually subsiding from the first stage to the second, take a look at the home.

00:00:49.108 --> 00:00:51.866
A furnished flat at $8 per week.

00:00:51.866 --> 00:00:58.823
It did not exactly beggar description, but it certainly had that word on the lookout for the mendicancy squad.

00:00:58.823 --> 00:01:07.737
In the vestibule below was a letter-box into which no letter would go, and an electric button from which no mortal finger could coax a ring.

00:01:07.737 --> 00:01:11.759
Also appertaining thereunto was a card bearing the name “Mr.

00:01:11.759 --> 00:01:13.895
James Dillingham Young.”

00:01:13.895 --> 00:01:22.535
The “Dillingham” had been flung to the breeze during a former period of prosperity when its possessor was being paid $30 per week.

00:01:22.535 --> 00:01:33.480
Now, when the income was shrunk to $20, the letters of “Dillingham” looked blurred, as though they were thinking seriously of contracting to a modest and unassuming D.

00:01:33.480 --> 00:01:34.466
But whenever Mr.

00:01:34.466 --> 00:01:41.092
James Dillingham Young came home and reached his flat above he was called “Jim” and greatly hugged by Mrs.

00:01:41.092 --> 00:01:43.893
James Dillingham Young, already

00:01:43.893 --> 00:01:45.866
introduced to you as Della.

00:01:45.866 --> 00:01:47.568
Which is all very good.

00:01:47.568 --> 00:01:52.352
Della finished her cry and attended to her cheeks with the powder rag.

00:01:52.352 --> 00:01:59.121
She stood by the window and looked out dully at a grey cat walking a grey fence in a grey backyard.

00:01:59.121 --> 00:02:06.094
Tomorrow would be Christmas Day, and she had only $1.87 with which to buy Jim a present.

00:02:06.094 --> 00:02:10.564
She had been saving every penny she could for months, with this result.

00:02:10.564 --> 00:02:13.124
Twenty dollars a week doesn’t go far.

00:02:13.124 --> 00:02:16.398
Expenses had been greater than she had calculated.

00:02:16.398 --> 00:02:17.689
They always are.

00:02:17.689 --> 00:02:22.174
Only $1.87 to buy a present for Jim.

00:02:22.174 --> 00:02:23.049
Her Jim.

00:02:23.049 --> 00:02:27.347
Many a happy hour she had spent planning for something nice for him.

00:02:27.347 --> 00:02:34.713
Something fine and rare and sterling—something just a little bit near to being worthy of the honour of being owned by Jim.

00:02:34.713 --> 00:02:37.833
There was a pier-glass between the windows of the room.

00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:41.730
Perhaps you have seen a pier-glass in an $8 flat.

00:02:41.730 --> 00:02:52.999
A very thin and very agile person may, by observing his reflection in a rapid sequence of longitudinal strips, obtain a fairly accurate conception of his looks.

00:02:52.999 --> 00:02:56.153
Della, being slender, had mastered the art.

00:02:56.153 --> 00:03:00.184
Suddenly she whirled from the window and stood before the glass.

00:03:00.184 --> 00:03:06.062
Her eyes were shining brilliantly, but her face had lost its colour within twenty seconds.

00:03:06.062 --> 00:03:10.500
Rapidly she pulled down her hair and let it fall to its full length.

00:03:11.115 --> 00:03:17.319
Now, there were two possessions of the James Dillingham Youngs in which they both took a mighty pride.

00:03:17.319 --> 00:03:21.954
One was Jim’s gold watch that had been his father’s and his grandfather’s.

00:03:21.954 --> 00:03:23.846
The other was Della’s hair.

00:03:23.846 --> 00:03:34.712
Had the Queen of Sheba lived in the flat across the airshaft, Della would have let her hair hang out the window some day to dry just to depreciate Her Majesty’s jewels and gifts.

00:03:34.712 --> 00:03:46.208
Had King Solomon been the janitor, with all his treasures piled up in the basement, Jim would have pulled out his watch every time he passed, just to see him pluck at his beard from envy.

00:03:46.208 --> 00:03:53.013
So now Della’s beautiful hair fell about her, rippling and shining like a cascade of brown waters.

00:03:53.013 --> 00:03:57.573
It reached below her knee and made itself almost a garment for her.

00:03:57.573 --> 00:04:00.882
And then she did it up again nervously and quickly.

00:04:00.882 --> 00:04:07.164
Once she faltered for a minute and stood still while a tear or two splashed on the worn red carpet.

00:04:07.164 --> 00:04:11.220
On went her old brown jacket; on went her old brown hat.

00:04:11.220 --> 00:04:19.082
With a whirl of skirts and with the brilliant sparkle still in her eyes, she fluttered out the door and down the stairs to the street.

00:04:19.082 --> 00:04:21.701
Where she stopped the sign read: “Mme.

00:04:21.701 --> 00:04:22.753
Sofronie.

00:04:22.753 --> 00:04:24.733
Hair Goods of All Kinds.”

00:04:24.733 --> 00:04:28.718
One flight up Della ran, and collected herself, panting.

00:04:28.718 --> 00:04:34.047
Madame, large, too white, chilly, hardly looked the “Sofronie.”

00:04:34.047 --> 00:04:36.590
“Will you buy my hair?” asked Della.

00:04:36.590 --> 00:04:38.545
“I buy hair,” said Madame.

00:04:38.545 --> 00:04:42.125
“Take yer hat off and let’s have a sight at the looks of it.”

00:04:42.125 --> 00:04:44.580
Down rippled the brown cascade.

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.354
“Twenty dollars,” said Madame, lifting the mass with a practised hand.

00:04:49.354 --> 00:04:51.596
“Give it to me quick,” said Della.

00:04:51.596 --> 00:04:55.720
Oh, and the next two hours tripped by on rosy wings.

00:04:55.720 --> 00:04:57.634
Forget the hashed metaphor.

00:04:57.634 --> 00:05:00.698
She was ransacking the stores for Jim’s present.

00:05:00.698 --> 00:05:02.438
She found it at last.

00:05:02.438 --> 00:05:05.581
It surely had been made for Jim and no one else.

00:05:05.581 --> 00:05:11.028
There was no other like it in any of the stores, and she had turned all of them inside out.

00:05:11.028 --> 00:05:23.341
It was a platinum fob chain simple and chaste in design, properly proclaiming its value by substance alone and not by meretricious ornamentation—as all good things should do.

00:05:23.341 --> 00:05:25.481
It was even worthy of The Watch.

00:05:25.481 --> 00:05:29.150
As soon as she saw it she knew that it must be Jim’s.

00:05:29.150 --> 00:05:30.210
It was like him.

00:05:30.210 --> 00:05:34.084
Quietness and value—the description applied to both.

00:05:34.084 --> 00:05:39.825
Twenty-one dollars they took from her for it, and she hurried home with the 87 cents.

00:05:39.825 --> 00:05:45.612
With that chain on his watch Jim might be properly anxious about the time in any company.

00:05:45.612 --> 00:05:53.707
Grand as the watch was, he sometimes looked at it on the sly on account of the old leather strap that he used in place of a chain.

00:05:53.707 --> 00:05:59.084
When Della reached home her intoxication gave way a little to prudence and reason.

00:05:59.084 --> 00:06:06.999
She got out her curling irons and lighted the gas and went to work repairing the ravages made by generosity added to love.

00:06:06.999 --> 00:06:12.074
Which is always a tremendous task, dear friends—a mammoth task.

00:06:12.074 --> 00:06:19.943
Within forty minutes her head was covered with tiny, close-lying curls that made her look wonderfully like a truant schoolboy.

00:06:19.943 --> 00:06:24.866
She looked at her reflection in the mirror long, carefully, and critically.

00:06:24.866 --> 00:06:33.274
“If Jim doesn’t kill me,” she said to herself, “before he takes a second look at me, he’ll say I look like a Coney Island chorus girl.

00:06:33.274 --> 00:06:38.236
But what could I do—oh! what could I do with a dollar and eighty-seven cents?”

00:06:38.851 --> 00:06:45.828
At 7 o’clock the coffee was made and the frying-pan was on the back of the stove hot and ready to cook the chops.

00:06:45.828 --> 00:06:47.378
Jim was never late.

00:06:47.378 --> 00:06:54.118
Della doubled the fob chain in her hand and sat on the corner of the table near the door that he always entered.

00:06:54.118 --> 00:07:00.439
Then she heard his step on the stair away down on the first flight, and she turned white for just a moment.

00:07:00.439 --> 00:07:10.381
She had a habit for saying little silent prayers about the simplest everyday things, and now she whispered: “Please God, make him think I am still pretty.”

00:07:10.381 --> 00:07:13.577
The door opened and Jim stepped in and closed it.

00:07:13.577 --> 00:07:16.051
He looked thin and very serious.

00:07:16.051 --> 00:07:20.983
Poor fellow, he was only twenty-two—and to be burdened with a family!

00:07:20.983 --> 00:07:24.321
He needed a new overcoat and he was without gloves.

00:07:24.321 --> 00:07:29.634
Jim stopped inside the door, as immovable as a setter at the scent of quail.

00:07:29.634 --> 00:07:36.434
His eyes were fixed upon Della, and there was an expression in them that she could not read, and it terrified her.

00:07:36.434 --> 00:07:44.530
It was not anger, nor surprise, nor disapproval, nor horror, nor any of the sentiments that she had been prepared for.

00:07:44.530 --> 00:07:49.922
He simply stared at her fixedly with that peculiar expression on his face.

00:07:49.922 --> 00:07:52.743
Della wriggled off the table and went for him.

00:07:52.743 --> 00:07:56.910
“Jim, darling,” she cried, “don’t look at me that way.

00:07:56.910 --> 00:08:02.853
I had my hair cut off and sold because I couldn’t have lived through Christmas without giving you a present.

00:08:02.853 --> 00:08:06.011
It’ll grow out again—you won’t mind, will you?

00:08:06.011 --> 00:08:07.482
I just had to do it.

00:08:07.482 --> 00:08:09.792
My hair grows awfully fast.

00:08:09.792 --> 00:08:11.483
Say ‘Merry Christmas!’

00:08:11.483 --> 00:08:13.522
Jim, and let’s be happy.

00:08:13.522 --> 00:08:18.086
You don’t know what a nice—what a beautiful, nice gift I’ve got for you.”

00:08:18.086 --> 00:08:26.851
“You’ve cut off your hair?” asked Jim, laboriously, as if he had not arrived at that patent fact yet even after the hardest mental labor.

00:08:26.851 --> 00:08:29.448
“Cut it off and sold it,” said Della.

00:08:29.448 --> 00:08:31.980
“Don’t you like me just as well, anyhow?

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.070
I’m me without my hair, ain’t I?”

00:08:34.070 --> 00:08:36.657
Jim looked about the room curiously.

00:08:36.657 --> 00:08:41.520
“You say your hair is gone?” he said, with an air almost of idiocy.

00:08:41.520 --> 00:08:43.763
“You needn’t look for it,” said Della.

00:08:43.763 --> 00:08:47.832
“It’s sold, I tell you—sold and gone, too.

00:08:47.832 --> 00:08:49.770
It’s Christmas Eve, boy.

00:08:49.770 --> 00:08:52.069
Be good to me, for it went for you.

00:08:52.069 --> 00:08:59.944
Maybe the hairs of my head were numbered,” she went on with sudden serious sweetness, “but nobody could ever count my love for you.

00:08:59.944 --> 00:09:02.191
Shall I put the chops on, Jim?”

00:09:02.191 --> 00:09:05.302
Out of his trance Jim seemed quickly to wake.

00:09:05.302 --> 00:09:07.032
He enfolded his Della.

00:09:07.032 --> 00:09:13.844
For ten seconds let us regard with discreet scrutiny some inconsequential object in the other direction.

00:09:13.844 --> 00:09:18.400
Eight dollars a week or a million a year—what is the difference?

00:09:18.400 --> 00:09:21.990
A mathematician or a wit would give you the wrong answer.

00:09:21.990 --> 00:09:26.371
The magi brought valuable gifts, but that was not among them.

00:09:26.371 --> 00:09:29.742
This dark assertion will be illuminated later on.

00:09:29.742 --> 00:09:34.648
Jim drew a package from his overcoat pocket and threw it upon the table.

00:09:34.648 --> 00:09:38.552
“Don’t make any mistake, Dell,” he said, “about me.

00:09:38.552 --> 00:09:45.488
I don’t think there’s anything in the way of a haircut or a shave or a shampoo that could make me like my girl any less.

00:09:45.488 --> 00:09:50.361
But if you’ll unwrap that package you may see why you had me going a while at first.”

00:09:50.976 --> 00:09:54.494
White fingers and nimble tore at the string and paper.

00:09:54.494 --> 00:10:08.013
And then an ecstatic scream of joy; and then, alas! a quick feminine change to hysterical tears and wails, necessitating the immediate employment of all the comforting powers of the lord of the flat.

00:10:08.013 --> 00:10:15.621
For there lay The Combs—the set of combs, side and back, that Della had worshipped long in a Broadway window.

00:10:15.621 --> 00:10:23.440
Beautiful combs, pure tortoise shell, with jewelled rims—just the shade to wear in the beautiful vanished hair.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:31.147
They were expensive combs, she knew, and her heart had simply craved and yearned over them without the least hope of possession.

00:10:31.147 --> 00:10:37.220
And now, they were hers, but the tresses that should have adorned the coveted adornments were gone.

00:10:37.220 --> 00:10:46.461
But she hugged them to her bosom, and at length she was able to look up with dim eyes and a smile and say: “My hair grows so fast, Jim!”

00:10:46.461 --> 00:10:51.699
And then Della leaped up like a little singed cat and cried, “Oh, oh!”

00:10:51.699 --> 00:10:54.722
Jim had not yet seen his beautiful present.

00:10:54.722 --> 00:10:58.039
She held it out to him eagerly upon her open palm.

00:10:58.039 --> 00:11:03.886
The dull precious metal seemed to flash with a reflection of her bright and ardent spirit.

00:11:03.886 --> 00:11:05.839
“Isn’t it a dandy, Jim?

00:11:05.839 --> 00:11:08.304
I hunted all over town to find it.

00:11:08.304 --> 00:11:11.805
You’ll have to look at the time a hundred times a day now.

00:11:11.805 --> 00:11:13.035
Give me your watch.

00:11:13.035 --> 00:11:14.955
I want to see how it looks on it.”

00:11:14.955 --> 00:11:21.836
Instead of obeying, Jim tumbled down on the couch and put his hands under the back of his head and smiled.

00:11:21.836 --> 00:11:27.032
“Dell,” said he, “let’s put our Christmas presents away and keep ’em a while.

00:11:27.032 --> 00:11:29.497
They’re too nice to use just at present.

00:11:29.497 --> 00:11:32.973
I sold the watch to get the money to buy your combs.

00:11:32.973 --> 00:11:35.408
And now suppose you put the chops on.”

00:11:35.408 --> 00:11:43.311
The magi, as you know, were wise men—wonderfully wise men—who brought gifts to the Babe in the manger.

00:11:43.311 --> 00:11:46.456
They invented the art of giving Christmas presents.

00:11:46.456 --> 00:11:54.433
Being wise, their gifts were no doubt wise ones, possibly bearing the privilege of exchange in case of duplication.

00:11:54.433 --> 00:12:05.536
And here I have lamely related to you the uneventful chronicle of two foolish children in a flat who most unwisely sacrificed for each other the greatest treasures of their house.

00:12:05.536 --> 00:12:12.477
But in a last word to the wise of these days let it be said that of all who give gifts these two were the wisest.

00:12:12.477 --> 00:12:16.577
Of all who give and receive gifts, such as they are wisest.

00:12:16.577 --> 00:12:18.349
Everywhere they are wisest.

00:12:18.349 --> 00:12:19.641
They are the magi.
